{:schema 1
 :receipt/type :limit-pushdown-third-attempt
 :date "2026-08-02"
 :status :NOT-LANDED
 :adr "ADR-2608021000 §6-6-1"
 :where-the-code-is "arrangement branch agent/limit-pushdown-v2 (unmerged). main is unchanged; the LDBC runs pinned it via :local/root."

 :what-the-three-attempts-cost
 [{:attempt 1 :defect "driver-independent clauses re-derived once per value group -- IC02's 882-row [?person knows ?friend] x 7,355 date groups"
   :found-by "LDBC wall time (CPU 15min against a 4-5min norm)" :unit-tests "all passed" :outcome "landed, then reverted (arrangement 6a179a31)"}
  {:attempt 2 :defect "pruning erased the join keys the two halves match on -- IC08 joins on ?msg, which is in no output column, so base collapsed to one empty binding that matched everything and returned replies to other people's messages"
   :found-by "LDBC :answers-agree" :unit-tests "all passed -- every fixture happened to name its join keys in :find" :outcome "fixed, pinned as a test"}
  {:attempt 3 :defect "drives regardless of selectivity; IC08's answer is a handful of rows spread over 7,355 date groups, so the walk never fills the limit"
   :found-by "predicted before measuring, then confirmed" :unit-tests "all passed" :outcome "selectivity guard added from :clause-cardinality, correctness restored (answers-agree 4/4), speed unproven"}]

 :measured
 {:v2-no-guard {:answers-agree {:ic02 true :ic07 true :ic08 false :ic09 true}
                :ratios {:ic02 18.1 :ic07 4.7 :ic08 88.3 :ic09 11.3}}
  :v3-with-guard {:answers-agree {:ic02 true :ic07 true :ic08 true :ic09 true}
                  :ratios {:ic02 50.4 :ic07 13.9 :ic08 60.7 :ic09 16.4}}}

 :why-not-landed
 "Correct, and unproven. IC02 went 219.6 -> 287.5 ms raw (1.31x slower) and IC08 77.4 -> 60.1 (1.29x faster) between the two runs -- both smaller than the noise in the controls, so neither is attributable."

 :the-control-broke-and-that-corrects-an-earlier-claim
 "IC07 and IC09 are NOT driven in this harness -- they still sort in host code and their kotobase side is byte-identical between the two runs. They moved anyway: IC07 kotobase 6.4 -> 16.1 ms (2.52x slower) and IC09 1113 -> 1809 ms (1.63x slower), while Neo4j's IC07 went 1.4 -> 1.2 ms in the SAME process.

  So unchanged kotobase code got 1.6-2.5x slower while unchanged Neo4j code got slightly faster, interleaved in one JVM. The kb/neo ratio is NOT load-invariant at this precision.

  That corrects something earlier receipts leaned on. 2026-08-02-ldbc-snb-after-join-work.edn and -ic02-hypothesis-refuted.edn presented the ratio as the load-invariant column and compared it across runs -- IC09 85.3 -> 35.8 -> 21.6, IC08 97.5 -> 53.3 -> 38.7. The DIRECTION and rough size of those still stand (they are large and monotone across three runs), but they carry more uncertainty than those receipts claimed, and a 20-30% movement in that column means nothing.

  The durable products remain what they always were: counts. scan 143,270 -> 885 does not move."

 :what-is-established
 ["The join-key bug is fixed and pinned by a test whose fixture keeps its join key OUT of :find -- the shape every earlier fixture missed."
  "The selectivity guard restores answers-agree 4/4 and declines to drive IC08, which is what it was written to do."
  "Nothing about speed."]

 :next
 "Measure on a quiet machine, both pins in one window, with a control that is genuinely unchanged code on the kotobase side. If the win does not show, the feature is surface without payment and should stay out."

 :caveats
 ["15 samples per query, one run per version. The controls moving 1.6-2.5x is itself the finding about this machine."
  "arrangement main does NOT contain this. Nothing shipped."]

 :raw-v3 {:schema 1, :caveats ["Subset of SF1, not full SF1 (~10.5M elements) -- the subset rule is stated above and is deterministic." "kotobase has no ORDER BY/LIMIT in its query language; the top-20 step runs in harness Clojure over the join's full result, while Neo4j does it inside Cypher. Both are timed to the same final answer and the answers are asserted equal." "kotobase stores knows in both directions (no undirected edge) and reifies likes (no edge properties); Neo4j uses a native undirected match and a relationship property." "kotobase's number is a hot in-memory db with no encryption and no persistence; the persisted path additionally pays hydration, which is measured in ADR-2607310900 and the dag-shape receipt, not here." "Neo4j embedded has no pure in-memory mode; its temp on-disk store is read through the page cache after warmup." "Single machine, single JVM, no concurrency. NamiDB's published numbers are on different hardware -- only the ratio against the shared Neo4j yardstick is comparable, and only loosely." "IC07/IC09 agreement compares result SETS (likers / message ids) rather than ordered rows: both queries can have creationDate ties at the LIMIT-20 boundary, where the two engines are free to pick different rows. IC02/IC08 are compared as ordered rows. A reported disagreement is reported, never relaxed after the fact."], :kotobase {:quads 409877, :build-ms 1637.400666, :note "hot db via eng/transact -- same db value the persisted path hydrates to; no AES-GCM/content-addressing in this number", :ic02 {:p50-ms 287.463625, :p95-ms 734.02325, :p99-ms 734.02325, :mean-ms 337.71092500000003, :samples 15}, :ic07 {:p50-ms 16.118208, :p95-ms 198.684042, :p99-ms 198.684042, :mean-ms 37.95590833333334, :samples 15}, :ic08 {:p50-ms 60.111625, :p95-ms 184.978875, :p99-ms 184.978875, :mean-ms 64.96690273333334, :samples 15}, :ic09 {:p50-ms 1808.93, :p95-ms 3128.334375, :p99-ms 3128.334375, :mean-ms 1998.1285111333332, :samples 15}}, :receipt/type :ldbc-snb-in